Subject: speech: a persona's voices are its own, spoken by a provider that exists

A composed identity had two reachable answers to `speechSynthesis.getVoices()`
and both said something true about the host.

Measured through the shipped `macos-arm64` binary, seed 42 windows: 180
voices, `localService` true on all of them, beginning Albert, Alice, Alva,
Amelie -- byte-identical to the list this Mac returns with no profile at all,
under a persona whose `navigator.platform` says Win32. Give that profile the
catalogue's Windows voices instead and the answer becomes 0 voices, because
patch 0043 filters real registered voices by the profile's names and no
provider on a Mac is called "Microsoft David - English (United States)".

Neither is a machine. Real Windows Chrome returns 22 voices; this project's
own Windows capture records them, three local Microsoft en-GB and the
nineteen "Google <language>" network voices, and the macOS capture records
the same nineteen. An empty list is not a machine either: no desktop Chrome
reports one.

WHAT CHANGES

`GetVoices` now serves the profile's list. Where a real provider is that
voice -- same name, same language, same locality -- the provider's own record
is used verbatim, so a Windows persona on a Windows host keeps its native
identifiers, engine ids and event sets exactly as 0043 left them. Where it is
not, the page is shown the persona's voice and a real provider is chosen to
speak it: same language first, then the same primary subtag, then the first
provider the platform offered. A requested voice is dropped when there is no
provider at all, so a host with no speech at all still enumerates nothing.

`SpeakNow` then speaks through that provider. The page picked a name this
host has never heard of, and what reaches `TtsPlatform::Speak` is the
substitute's record, with its own name and native identifier, so synthesis
runs on a real engine and the start, word, sentence and end events are that
engine's. 0043's failure path is unchanged for a request that matches
nothing.

WHAT A PAGE CAN TELL

Not the substitution, by reading. `SpeechSynthesisVoice` exposes name,
`voiceURI`, `lang`, `localService` and `default`, all of which come from the
profile; `engine_id` and the native identifier are routing fields Blink never
surfaces. Not by listening either: a page cannot capture speech synthesis
output, which does not reach Web Audio or any capture path.

By timing, in principle. A voice the profile marks remote is answered by a
local engine, so `onstart` arrives without a network round trip. That is the
one residual, it is bounded to profiles that claim remote voices, and it is
a better answer than either of the two this replaces.

WHY NOT FABRICATE THE EVENTS INSTEAD

Because that is what patch 0037 did and 0043 removed: start and end invented
on a character-count timer, with no audio and no provider. The difference
here is that synthesis is real and only the name is the persona's, so there
is no timer, no synthetic completion, and nothing to keep in step with an
engine that is not running.

The catalogue moves with this: every platform's voice options now carry the
nineteen network voices that both captures record, the Windows en-GB option
is the capture verbatim, and the option served for an accept-languages list
the table does not key on is that platform's own set rather than the host's.

Verification: V1 here, with four tests over the composed list and the
substitution -- a voice with no provider of its own is still listed, the
host's own names never appear under a profile, speaking reaches the platform
as the substitute, and the substitute follows the language. 0043's three
tests are unchanged and still pass, which is what says the verbatim path is
intact.

